Your job is to produce a single `<screen_subcomparch>` block containing
five sections in a fixed order: a role-level technical specification,
its public surface, its private surface, its process inventory, and
its dependencies.

# Output format

Emit exactly one `<screen_subcomparch>` block with these children in
this order: `<technical-specification>` → `<public-surface>` →
`<private-surface>` → `<processes>` → `<dependencies>` →
`<references>` (optional). Example (abbreviated):

    <screen_subcomparch>
      <technical-specification>
    A LiveView mounted at `/plans`. On mount, loads the plan roster
    from Billing and assigns it whole; `handle_event("select_plan",
    ...)` starts a draft via Billing's boundary export and navigates
    to the payment-details route with the draft id.
      </technical-specification>
      <public-surface>
    ```
    mount(_params, _session, socket) -> {:ok, socket}
    handle_event("select_plan", %{"plan_id" => id}, socket) ->
      {:noreply, Phoenix.LiveView.push_navigate(socket, ...)}
    ```
      </public-surface>
      <private-surface>
    ```
    load_plan_roster() -> [Plan.t()] — wraps Billing's list call with
    this LiveView's own sort/filter defaults.
    ```
      </private-surface>
      <processes>
        <process name="plan_select_live">
          <category>local</category>
          <purpose>The LiveView's own process — holds the mounted
          socket's assigns between events; per-connection, no
          cross-node coordination.</purpose>
        </process>
      </processes>
      <dependencies>
      </dependencies>
    </screen_subcomparch>

# Rules

## Structure

* Emit **exactly one** `<screen_subcomparch>` root block. The five
  children **must appear in this order**: `<technical-specification>`
  → `<public-surface>` → `<private-surface>` → `<processes>` →
  `<dependencies>` → `<references>` (omit entirely when nothing is
  attached).

## Fragment sections

* `<technical-specification>` names the mount behavior, the events
  this leaf handles, and which backend calls each handler makes —
  narrow the parent collection's own technical specification to this
  leaf's slice.
* `<public-surface>` names every `mount`/`handle_event`/`handle_info`
  entry point sibling leaves or the router need to know about, with
  real signatures. `<private-surface>` is this leaf's own helpers.
  **Every backend call this leaf makes must be grounded here** — name
  which backend pubapi entry it calls, not just that a call happens.

## Processes

* Every LiveView leaf is its own process — declare it here, `category:
  local` (per-connection, no cross-node coordination) is the default
  for an ordinary mounted view. Use `singleton` only for a
  collection-wide coordinator with no per-connection shape, and
  `sharded` only for a genuinely keyed worker pool this leaf owns
  (rare at this tier). Add `<guardrails>` when the techspec or private
  surface describes unbounded input this process absorbs.

## Dependencies

* `<dependencies>` lists sibling leaves within the same screen
  collection this one calls into (a shared mount helper, a shared
  data-loading leaf), each `<dep to="..."/>` with a real id from the
  list above.

## References

* `<references>` is optional — omit entirely when nothing is attached.

## Meta-rules

* Do not include commentary about what you are doing or how you
  arrived at the design. Output only the `<screen_subcomparch>` block.
* Unescaped `&` and `<` inside fragment-section text are tolerated.
* Do not emit `<policies>`, `<subcomponents>`, or `<sub-dependencies>`
  — leaves in this tier don't introduce new policies and can't
  decompose further.
